.TH FDRD 8 "23 August 2026" "fdr 1.4.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
fdrd \- record configured Linux ftrace events
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fdrd
[\fB\-fjnvV\fR]
[\fB\-a\fR \fIaddress\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIport\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR \fIconfig-directory\fR]
[\fB\-d\fR \fItracefs-root\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fdrd
creates isolated ftrace instances, enables configured tracepoints, and
optionally appends events to files. One worker process is used per
configuration file.
.PP
Configuration files are regular files named
.B *.conf
directly under
.BR /etc/fdr.d .
Each file must begin with exactly one
.B instance
directive. Configuration is validated before workers start.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-a " address"
IPv4 address for HTTP health and metrics. Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
.TP
.BI \-c " directory"
Configuration directory. Default:
.BR /etc/fdr.d .
.TP
.BI \-d " directory"
Tracefs root. The
.B instances
component is appended automatically.
.TP
.B \-f
Remain in the foreground. Required under systemd and in containers.
.TP
.B \-j
Write one JSON object per log line.
.TP
.B \-n
Validate configuration and exit without accessing tracefs.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
HTTP port. Default: 9119. Zero disables HTTP.
.TP
.B \-v
Increase verbosity.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the program version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.BI "instance " "name [buffer-size]"
Create an isolated trace instance. Sizes are bytes with optional
.BR k ,
.BR m ,
or
.B g
suffixes. FDR converts the value to the kernel's KiB-per-CPU unit.
.TP
.BI "modprobe " module
Load a module without invoking a shell.
.TP
.BI "enable " "subsystem/event [filter]"
Apply an optional ftrace filter and enable an event.
.I subsystem/all
enables a complete subsystem.
.TP
.BI "disable " subsystem/event
Disable an event or
.IR subsystem/all .
.TP
.BI "minfree " percentage
Drop trace data when available space on the output filesystem is at or below
this percentage. The default is 5.
.TP
.BI "saveto " "absolute-path [maximum-size]"
Append trace data to a regular file. At the optional limit, use the instance's
logrotate configuration or preserve one fallback generation with a
.B .1
suffix.
.SH HTTP
.TP
.B /healthz
Liveness of the parent event loop.
.TP
.B /readyz
Readiness of configured probes, persistent collectors, and trace integrity.
Storage-protection drops do not change readiness; monitor
.B fdr_bytes_dropped_total
separately.
.TP
.B /metrics
Prometheus text metrics, including bytes written or dropped, rotations, probe
and write failures, worker state, and cumulative tracefs overruns or dropped
events. Newly observed trace loss makes readiness false.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Validate the complete configuration directory. Invalid new configuration is
rejected without disturbing current workers. A valid reload stops the current
workers and trace instances before creating the replacement set, so it can
briefly interrupt capture.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Collector processes reopen their output files. Send it to all processes in the
service after external log rotation.
.TP
.B SIGTERM, SIGINT
Stop workers, remove trace instances, and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/fdr.d/*.conf
Instance configuration.
.TP
.B /etc/logrotate.d/<instance>
Optional rotation policy.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/tracing/instances
Preferred tracefs instance directory.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/debug/tracing/instances
Legacy fallback.
.SH SECURITY
The daemon normally runs as root and controls host-kernel tracing. Configuration
write access must be restricted to administrators. HTTP has no built-in
authentication or TLS and binds to loopback by default.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR logrotate (8),
.BR modprobe (8),
.BR systemd.service (5)
